--TEST--
An optional shape field read under a guard answers `null` where it is absent, and the same read written without one throws catchably — `rule:types/shape-type` and `rule:types/erased-member-access`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/shape-type`: `{a?: string}` proves the field's *type* and not
// its presence, so the two spellings of the same read answer differently.
// `??`, `isset` and `empty` are the ones that ask whether the key is there;
// everything else takes `rule:types/erased-member-access`'s throw.
class Opt {
    // Under a guard the read answers `?string`, so an absent `a` is the
    // `null` the `??` tests and never reaches the default's own operand.
    public static function guarded({a?: string, b: int} $p): string {
        return $p->a ?? "default";
    }

    // The same field, the same value, no guard: absence is a checked,
    // catchable throw naming the field and the concrete class.
    public static function unguarded({a?: string, b: int} $p): string {
        try {
            return $p->a;
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return $e->message;
        }
    }

    // `isset` asks the same question and answers it without a default.
    public static function asked({a?: string, b: int} $p): bool {
        return isset($p->a);
    }
}

// A value with no `a` at all satisfies the shape, the key being optional —
// and one that carries an `a` satisfies it too, so both reach every read
// above through the same parameter.
echo Opt::guarded({b: 1}), "\n";
echo Opt::guarded({a: "here", b: 1}), "\n";
echo Opt::unguarded({b: 1}), "\n";
echo Opt::unguarded({a: "here", b: 1}), "\n";
echo Opt::asked({b: 1}) ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
echo Opt::asked({a: "here", b: 1}) ? "yes" : "no", "\n";
--EXPECT--
default
here
`$shape{b}` has no field `a`
here
no
yes
